# Rank 1224 / CVE-2024-37358: Apache James's IMAP frame reader consumed a
# request line one byte at a time with no cap on how long that line could
# grow (CWE-400, GHSA-c5wf-8fj8-4j3q). NettyImapRequestLineReader.nextChar()
# increments a per-connection `read` counter on every byte pulled off the
# Netty ChannelBuffer, but the vulnerable form never compares that counter
# against anything -- a pre-authentication client that keeps sending bytes
# without a CRLF or a declared IMAP literal drives the frame decoder's
# cumulation buffer arbitrarily large, since ImapRequestLineReader's tag/
# command/argument parsers all loop by repeatedly calling nextChar() until a
# delimiter appears.
#
# Pool commit b2f3c06edf carries no flag; read its diff per Step 0's note
# that flags are not the only source of pool errors, and confirmed it is the
# real, targeted fix: it touches exactly IMAPServer.java,
# ImapRequestFrameDecoder.java, NettyImapRequestLineReader.java and that
# class's test, all under
# server/protocols/protocols-imap4/src/main/java/org/apache/james/imapserver/netty,
# threading a new maxFrameLength constructor parameter down to nextChar(),
# which now throws once `read > maxFrameLength`. The same commit also scopes
# the literal-size limit itself to a small fixed default
# (UNAUTHENTICATE_LITERAL_MAX_SIZE, 8192) for any session that is not yet
# authenticated, but the line-length cap modelled here is the one added to
# nextChar() -- the unbounded-accumulation shape a rule can express, as
# opposed to the pre-auth-vs-post-auth trust distinction the second half adds.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap, the same gap rank 1192 already isolated and entered
# in ENGINE-GAPS.md. vyql match on a minimal pair reproducing nextChar()
# verbatim from both revisions labels nothing at all -- no source, sink, or
# issue on either one -- and vyql resolve shows every call inside the method
# (buffer.readable, the constructor's own class/function context) terminating
# unresolved, not a known source/sink/control. vyql scan --cache off
# --max-ram 4GB over the real protocols-imap4 module (23 java files, both
# revisions) reports zero findings on either side.
#
# Checked for reuse first: code.UnboundedAllocationReview (VYQL-DOS-041,
# CWE_400/770) is the right concept by CWE and by its own review text --
# "review allocation sizes derived from parsed counts for per-item limits,
# aggregate consistency checks, and overflow" describes exactly this counter
# -- but every language that binds it does so from a dedicated per-language
# analysis fact (C's allocation.unbounded_accumulated_count, Rust's
# rustReview == "unbounded_next_await", PHP's phpReview ==
# "unbounded_report_period"), never from presenceNode's flat context bag
# alone, and Java has none, confirmed at rank 1192 and unchanged since: the
# only literal names available in nextChar() are "read", "buffer" and
# "readable" -- ordinary field and method names that also appear in any
# byte-counting read loop, safe or not -- and presenceNode's context bag has
# no notion of loop structure or of an identity between the counter that is
# incremented and a bound it is (or is not) compared against, the two facts
# C's dedicated analysis correlates for the allocation case. This is not a
# new gap to enter separately: it is the same missing Java analogue of
# ccUnboundedAccumulatedAllocationObservations that rank 1192's
# ENGINE-GAPS.md entry already names, so no new entry is added here -- see
# that entry for the fix shape. No definition change attempted, per this
# loop's instruction not to do engine work.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions reject VYQL-DOS-041.

test "rank1224: nextChar increments a byte counter with no upper bound on line length"
  lang java
  file NettyImapRequestLineReader.java
  reject VYQL-DOS-041
  reject_review code.UnboundedAllocationReview
  code
  ```
  public class NettyImapRequestLineReader extends AbstractNettyImapRequestLineReader {

      private final ChannelBuffer buffer;
      private int read = 0;
      private final int maxLiteralSize;

      public NettyImapRequestLineReader(Channel channel, ChannelBuffer buffer, boolean retry, int maxLiteralSize) {
          super(channel, retry);
          this.buffer = buffer;
          this.maxLiteralSize = maxLiteralSize;
      }

      @Override
      public char nextChar() throws DecodingException {
          if (!nextSeen) {
              int next;

              if (buffer.readable()) {
                  next = buffer.readByte();
                  read++;
              } else {
                  throw new NotEnoughDataException();
              }
              nextSeen = true;
              nextChar = (char) next;
          }
          return nextChar;
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1224: nextChar rejects once the counter passes maxFrameLength"
  lang java
  file NettyImapRequestLineReader.java
  reject VYQL-DOS-041
  reject_review code.UnboundedAllocationReview
  code
  ```
  public class NettyImapRequestLineReader extends AbstractNettyImapRequestLineReader {

      private final ChannelBuffer buffer;
      private int read = 0;
      private final int maxLiteralSize;
      private final int maxFrameLength;

      public NettyImapRequestLineReader(Channel channel, ChannelBuffer buffer, boolean retry, int maxLiteralSize, int maxFrameLength) {
          super(channel, retry);
          this.buffer = buffer;
          this.maxLiteralSize = maxLiteralSize;
          this.maxFrameLength = maxFrameLength;
      }

      @Override
      public char nextChar() throws DecodingException {
          if (!nextSeen) {
              int next;

              if (buffer.readable()) {
                  next = buffer.readByte();
                  read++;
                  if (read > maxFrameLength) {
                      throw new DecodingException(HumanReadableText.FAILED, "Line length exceeded.");
                  }
              } else {
                  throw new NotEnoughDataException();
              }
              nextSeen = true;
              nextChar = (char) next;
          }
          return nextChar;
      }
  }
  ```
